  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">LANDSCAPE APPROACH TO CONSERVE ASIAN ELEPHANTS IN NORTHEAST INDIA WITH PARTICULAR FOCUS TO ASSAM AND MEGHALAYA, INDIA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE GOAL OF THIS PROJECT IS TO CONSERVE WILD ASIAN ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITATS IN NORTHEAST INDIA VIA EMPIRICAL RESEARCH, FACILITATION OF HUMAN ELEPHANT COEXISTENCE (HECX), AND OUTREACH AND SUPPORT TO STAKEHOLDERS. THE PROJECT HAS FOUR MAJOR OBJECTIVES, WHICH IMPACT BOTH LANDSCAPE AND LOCAL LEVEL CONSERVATION IN NORTHEAST INDIA (1) TO UNDERSTAND DISTRIBUTION, MOVEMENT, AND GENETIC CONNECTIVITY OF ELEPHANT POPULATIONS AND USE THAT DATA TO INFORM CONSERVATION DECISION MAKING AND POPULATION MANAGEMENT (2) TO MITIGATE HUMAN ELEPHANT CONFLICT (HEC) AND FACILITATE HECX (3) TO PROVIDE OUTREACH AND CAPACITY DEVELOPMENT AND (4) TO PROVIDE SUPPORT TO FOREST DEPARTMENT AND PROTECTED AREA OFFICIALS. PROJECT ACTIVITIES INCLUDE (1) NON 1 INVASIVE ELEPHANT DNA COLLECTION, EXTRACTION, SEQUENCING, MULTI LOCUS MICROSATELLITE GENOTYPING, ANALYSIS TO ASSESS GENETIC DIVERSITY, POPULATION GENETIC STRUCTURE, AND GENE FLOW (2) OCCUPANCY MODELLING OF ELEPHANT DISTRIBUTION IN MEGHALAYA USING DUNG COUNTS, PHOTOGRAPHIC MARK RECAPTURE, AND GENETIC SAMPLING (3) ASSESSMENT OF INTERSTATE HABITAT CONNECTIVITY AMONG MEGHALAYA, ARUNACHAL PRADESH, MIZORAM, ASSAM, AND NAGALAND WITH GIS ANALYSIS, GROUND TRUTHING, SURVEYS OF LOCAL RESIDENTS, AND CONSULTATIONS WITH STATE FOREST AND ADMINISTRATIVE OFFICIALS (4) DEVELOPING A HABITAT RESTORATIO</narrative>
  </description>
